6431aca0", "title": "Design of a Query Expression for Multi-Dimensional Graph Warehousing", "work_id": "wrk_484dd41d369106cbe1f556f3"}, {"abstract_preview": "Graphs are clear and straightforward representation forms. In machine learning, tree-shaped representations might be regarded as typical graphs and there are nodes and links in a tree. Graphs are a kind of data structure to represent a set of objects (vertices Vertices or nodes) and their relationships (link edges).", "access_status": "metadata_only", "artifact_ids": ["art_1b0ca7eb041c3ed8656839a8"], "authors": ["Zhang, Tongyi"], "full_text_artifact_ids": [], "publication_year": 2026, "source_id": "springer", "source_record_id": "src_c665eb240c709cdaccf784f4", "title": "Graph Neural Networks (GNNs)", "work_id": "wrk_7fce32b3bf09223954dc2513"}, {"abstract_preview": "Drug-Drug Interactions (DDIs) often cause significant adverse events, making early identification crucial, especially with rising polypharmacy. While most methods focus on binary prediction, multiclass DDI prediction is more advantageous as it identifies specific interaction types. However, this is challenging due to class imbalance, with many rare interaction types compared to dominant ones. We propose a multiclass DDI prediction framework comprising three components: Stage 1 involves feature e", "access_status": "metadata_only", "artifact_ids": ["art_dbb3ec0d007fd6497d016284"], "authors": ["Marisetti, Sathya Abhijna", "Maity, Maitreya", "PriyaKumar, U. Deva"], "full_text_artifact_ids": [], "publication_year": 2027, "source_id": "springer", "source_record_id": "src_dd4d44541b05d4461ef6f616", "title": "MambaDDI: A Hybrid State Space Framework for Multiclass Drug–Drug Interaction Prediction", "work_id": "wrk_ed4cb3cfc805567898a6ac35"}, {"abstract_preview": "Detecting adverse Drug-Drug Interactions (DDIs) is critical for patient safety. Graph-based models have advanced DDI prediction by integrating heterogeneous biomedical knowledge. However, most fail to account for patient heterogeneity. Recent work emphasizes that population factors (e.g. age, gender, weight) strongly influence drug outcomes. We built a Neo4j knowledge graph by integrating Canada Vigilance ADR reports, DrugBank, and other biomedical sources. Nodes represent drugs, targets (genes/", "access_status": "metadata_only", "artifact_ids": ["art_8d29f8ae01f88ebe6b0c20ab"], "authors": ["Fard, Mohadeseh Akhavan", "Kafaie, Somayeh", "Rajabi, Enayat"], "full_text_artifact_ids": [], "publication_year": 2026, "source_id": "springer", "source_record_id": "src_4e0fb5bce67bf9442c1ff6e3", "title": "DDI Prediction Using Patient Demographics in Knowledge Graphs", "work_id": "wrk_ebe6f9c3ca9e72cfb9f17958"}, {"abstract_preview": "For deep learning-based object identification systems to be more accurate and efficient, feature selection is essential. High-dimensional feature spaces frequently contain redundant or unnecessary data, which raises the danger of overfitting and increases computational expense. In this work, we compare two widely used nature-inspired metaheuristic algorithms for feature reduction in deep learning pipelines: Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) and Grey Wolf Optimizer (GWO). In order to find an idea", "access_status": "metadata_only", "artifact_ids": ["art_c4a57787c78039e0fe03f8dc"], "authors": ["Tuan, Nguyen Minh", "Meesad, Phayung"], "full_text_artifact_ids": [], "publication_year": 2027, "source_id": "springer", "source_record_id": "src_1a75f1d7b9720b22f71d56b0", "title": "Analysis of Particle Swarm Optimization and Grey Wolf Optimizer for Feature Reduction in Deep Learning-Based Object Recognition Tasks", "work_id": "wrk_efdcfa1ec5d62fe1fff64e05"}, {"abstract_preview": "Graph neural networks (GNNs) have become a powerful framework for analyzing structured data in the form of graphs, with applications spanning diverse fields such as social networks, biology, and recommender systems. This survey explores methodology, development, and advances in GNN architecture. We methodically survey the major classes of GNNs, including convolutional GNNs (ConvGNNs), spatial–temporal graph neural networks (STGNNs), recurrent-based GNNs (RecGNNs), and graph autoencoders (GAEs).", "access_status": "metadata_only", "artifact_ids": ["art_8eb173b983f525f0fa01d20d"], "authors": ["Maghdid, Sozan S.", "Rashid, Tarik A.", "Askar, Shavan K."], "full_text_artifact_ids": [], "publication_year": 2025, "source_id": "springer", "source_record_id": "src_758fdc3a6123b1d3c4f7a893", "title": "Graph neural networks: Historical backgrounds, present revolutions, and conventionalization for the future", "work_id": "wrk_9e12ca47229eaa43237eea6f"}, {"abstract_preview": "Contemporary CI/CD practices typically address vulnerability prevention and intrusion mitigation as disjoint activities, which may result in residual risk even after container images pass pre-deployment checks. Pre-deployment scanners can overlook vulnerabilities obscured by image layering or insufficient metadata, whereas runtime detection mechanisms commonly rely on single-source observations and thus fail to capture multi-step exploit chains. This work presents Janus , a coordinated framework", "access_status": "metadata_only", "artifact_ids": ["art_d8da80791e49258fb27bcb6d"], "authors": ["Majumder, Shafayat Hossain", "Majumdar, Suryadipta", "Jajodia, Sourov", "Jean-Baptiste, Patrick"], "full_text_artifact_ids": [], "publication_year": 2027, "source_id": "springer", "source_record_id": "src_53fcc077299e33bb92b5753a", "title": "Janus: Coordinating Vulnerability Prevention and Exploit-Chain Mitigation for Containerized CI/CD Pipelines", "work_id": "wrk_0b332e03df476b538f7685ee"}], "retrieval_status": "HAS_RESULTS", "source_id": "springer", "succeeded": true, "summary": "数据库检索召回 8 个候选作品", "warnings": ["S1-fb1: fallback_not_applicable; base strategy hit", "S2-fb1: fallback_not_applicable; base strategy hit", "S3-fb1: fallback_not_applicable; base strategy hit"]}

